drills.solutionTail:
// ──────────────────────────── types ──────────────────────────────────────

abstract class Notifier
{
    public abstract string Channel { get; }
    public virtual string Format(string message) => $"[{Channel}] {message}";
}

class EmailNotifier : Notifier
{
    public override string Channel => "email";
}

class SmsNotifier : Notifier
{
    public override string Channel => "sms";

    public override string Format(string message)
    {
        var text = base.Format(message);
        return text.Length > 30 ? text[..29] + "…" : text;
    }
}

drills.explanation:
`Channel` is abstract, so each subclass must supply it; `SmsNotifier` builds on the base format with `base.Format`:

```csharp
public override string Format(string message)
{
    var text = base.Format(message);
    return text.Length > 30 ? text[..29] + "…" : text;
}
```

Because `Format` is virtual, the test that calls it through a `Notifier` reference still gets the SMS rules. Declaring it `new` instead of `override` would make that test fail.
